\begin{document}

\title{More Code, Slower Tangle: AI Adoption Across Twenty MOSIP Core Services}

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------
% TODO: fill in authors
%------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\author{Author One}
\affiliation{%
  \institution{Carnegie Mellon University Africa}
  \city{Kigali}
  \country{Rwanda}}
\email{author@example.org}

\renewcommand{\shortauthors}{Author One}

\begin{abstract}
AI coding assistants are widely argued to trade short-term velocity for
long-term maintainability. We test this inside a single organisation: twenty
core Java services of MOSIP, the open-source digital identity platform deployed
by several national governments. We reconstruct \num{326} repository-months of
history from git, the GitHub API and \num{286} monthly SonarQube snapshots, and
detect AI adoption from commit trailers, agent configuration files and bot
accounts without relying on self-report.

MOSIP adopted two distinct classes of AI tool seven months apart --- an AI code
\emph{reviewer} organisation-wide in November 2025, and AI code \emph{writers}
from March 2026 --- which we treat as separate interventions. All measures are
reported in absolute terms, without normalisation. After coding-agent adoption,
code volume grew in \sig{all 16} repositories with quality data (median
$+11\%$, $p<0.0001$). Cognitive complexity rose in 14 of 16 repositories
($+2\%$, $p=0.005$) and code smells in 13 of 16 ($+5\%$, $p=0.011$), while
technical debt, bugs and vulnerabilities did not move and duplication fell
($-3\%$, $p=0.024$). The quality burden therefore grew, and grew more slowly
than the codebase producing it. Following review-bot adoption, human review per
pull request \emph{rose} from \num{1.43} to \num{1.80} ($p=0.009$): the bot
was additive, not substitutive.

We report these as descriptive, not causal. Adoption was near-simultaneous
across repositories ($\mathrm{corr}(\evt,t)=0.98$), so event time is nearly
collinear with calendar time and a shared shock cannot be separated from a
treatment effect --- an asymmetry that leaves our null results more robust than
any claim about activity levels.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{AI coding assistants, code quality, mining software repositories,
digital public infrastructure, event study}

\maketitle

%==============================================================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The claim that AI coding assistants trade short-term speed for long-term
maintainability is now widely repeated and has recent empirical support. He
et~al.~\cite{he2026speed} report that Cursor adoption raises velocity while
worsening complexity growth across a large population of open-source projects,
estimating an \emph{average} effect using difference-in-differences against
matched controls.

This work asks a narrower question with a different unit of analysis: what
happens inside \emph{one} organisation? MOSIP is digital public infrastructure
--- the identity platform deployed by several national governments --- so its
code is long-lived, security-sensitive, and maintained by a stable engineering
organisation rather than a churning volunteer pool. If AI assistance degrades
maintainability anywhere, this is a setting where the consequences matter.

MOSIP also proves to be an unusually clean natural setting, for a reason that
emerged only during data collection: the organisation adopted \textbf{two
different classes of AI tool, seven months apart}. An AI code \emph{reviewer}
arrived first, organisation-wide; AI code \emph{writers} followed. These are
distinct interventions with distinct predicted effects, and prior work
generally collapses them.

\paragraph{Research questions}
\begin{description}
  \item[RQ1 (Volume)] Did the amount of code, and the rate at which it is
    produced, change after AI coding agents were adopted?
  \item[RQ2 (Quality)] Did internal code quality --- complexity, code smells,
    technical debt --- change after adoption?
  \item[RQ3 (Review practice)] Did an AI code-review bot substitute for human
    review effort, or add to it?
\end{description}

%==============================================================================
\section{Methods}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Repository selection}
\label{sec:selection}

The MOSIP GitHub organisation holds \num{109} public repositories, \num{97} of
them neither archived nor forks. Ranking by stars is uninformative here --- the
whole organisation spans 4 to 87 stars --- and promotes artefacts with no
analysable code, such as \texttt{documentation} (\SI{794}{\mega\byte} of prose)
and the empty \texttt{mosip} meta-repository.

We instead ranked by \textbf{JVM source volume} obtained from the GitHub
languages API, after excluding by name mocks, test harnesses, deployment and
infrastructure repositories, configuration repositories and documentation. That
left \num{36} candidate core services, of which we took the \textbf{top 20 by
source volume}, totalling \SI{47.2}{\mega\byte} of JVM source. Checkout size
was rejected as a criterion because it is inflated by history and binaries:
\texttt{packet-manager} occupies \SI{188}{\mega\byte} on disk but holds only
\SI{0.51}{\mega\byte} of Java.

\subsection{Data collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Three independent sources were collected in September 2026 over a window of
2025-01 to 2026-08 (Table~\ref{tab:data}).

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Data sources and coverage.}
\label{tab:data}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llr@{}}
\toprule
Source & Extracted & Volume \\
\midrule
Git history   & commits, lines $\pm$, contributors & \num{6786} commits \\
              &                                    & \num{3188107} lines added \\
GitHub API    & pull requests, reviews, issues     & \num{5603} PRs \\
              &                                    & \num{17090} review records \\
              &                                    & \num{1222} issues \\
SonarQube 26.8& ncloc, cognitive complexity, code  & \num{286} snapshots \\
              & smells, technical debt, bugs,      & (\num{285}/\num{326} \\
              & vulnerabilities, duplication       & repo-months, 87\%) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

SonarQube reports only on a checked-out working tree, so history was
reconstructed by checking out the last commit of each month and re-analysing
the project in full --- \num{286} independent analyses on a dedicated Linux
host. Months in which nothing landed on the integration branch have no
snapshot, accounting for nearly all of the 13\% shortfall. Analysis ran on
source without compilation, matching the procedure of~\cite{he2026speed}.

Velocity is counted across \emph{all} branches (\texttt{git log -{}-all}),
because MOSIP follows gitflow and \texttt{master} held only \num{2025} of
\num{6786} commits in the window; quality snapshots necessarily follow a single
integration branch. The two halves therefore describe different commit sets ---
``all work performed'' versus ``quality of the shippable tree'' --- and are not
strictly commensurable.

\subsection{Detecting adoption}
\label{sec:detection}

Adoption was not self-reported. Three independent signal families were matched
against every repository: commit-message trailers and identities
(\texttt{Co-Authored-By: Claude}, \texttt{cursoragent}), agent configuration
files committed to the tree (\texttt{CLAUDE.md}, \texttt{.cursor/},
\texttt{AGENTS.md}), and bot accounts acting on GitHub. Detection yielded
\num{4288} AI-attributable actions (Table~\ref{tab:tools}).

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Detected AI tools and organisation-wide adoption.}
\label{tab:tools}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrl@{}}
\toprule
Tool & Class & Actions & Adoption \\
\midrule
CodeRabbit    & reviews code & \num{4141} & 2025-11 (14/19 repos) \\
Cursor        & writes code  & \num{64}   & 2026-03 -- 2026-08 \\
OpenAI Codex  & writes code  & \num{48}   & 2026-04 -- 2026-06 \\
Claude Code   & writes code  & \num{35}   & 2026-06 -- 2026-08 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The gap between a repository's review-bot and coding-agent adoption has median
\textbf{7 months} (range 4--9). RQ1 and RQ2 are therefore evaluated against
\textbf{coding-agent} adoption and RQ3 against \textbf{review-bot} adoption.
Collapsing the two attributes code-generation effects to the arrival of a
commenting tool.

\subsection{Analysis design}
\label{sec:design}

Each repository's timeline is re-indexed to event time,
\begin{equation}
  \evt_{it} = t - a_i ,
  \label{eq:eventtime}
\end{equation}
where $a_i$ is repository $i$'s own adoption month, and the twenty series are
pooled by $\evt$.

\textbf{All measures are reported raw.} No normalisation is applied: no ratio
to a pre-adoption baseline, and no division by code size. Figures use
logarithmic axes where repositories span orders of magnitude (\texttt{ncloc}
ranges from \num{7931} to \num{355811}), but that is a display choice and
leaves the values untouched.

Every test is a \emph{within-repository} comparison, so each repository serves
as its own control and differences in scale between repositories do not
confound the tests themselves. Scale does dominate the pooled box plots, where
the height of each box is largely between-repository size rather than change
over time; the per-repository view in Figure~\ref{fig:perrepo} is provided for
that reason.

Hypothesis tests are computed at the \textbf{repository level}: each repository
contributes one pre-adoption median and one post-adoption median, giving 16--19
independent pairs compared by Wilcoxon signed-rank. Pooling repository-months
would inflate $n$ to roughly \num{300} and overstate significance, since months
within a repository are strongly autocorrelated. The incomplete current month
is excluded throughout.

%==============================================================================
\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Code volume}
\label{sec:volume}

Table~\ref{tab:volume} splits at coding-agent adoption, with pre
$=\evt\in[-6,-1]$ and post $=\evt\in[+1,+3]$.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Code volume, raw. Repository-level paired medians, Wilcoxon signed-rank.}
\label{tab:volume}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Measure & pre & post & ratio & $p$ & $n$ & up \\
\midrule
Code size (ncloc)       & \num{45362} & \num{50531} & 1.11 & \sig{$<$.0001} & 16 & \sig{16/16} \\
Lines removed / month   & 403   & 501   & 1.24 & \sig{.007} & 17 & \sig{14/17} \\
Lines added / month     & \num{1583} & \num{3098} & 1.96 & .145 & 17 & 10/17 \\
Lines added / commit    & 243.8 & 435.7 & 1.79 & .244 & 17 & 9/17 \\
Commits / month         & 11.0  & 12.0  & 1.09 & .287 & 17 & 11/17 \\
Contributors / month    & 4.5   & 3.5   & 0.78 & .726 & 17 & 5/17 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Finding 1} Code volume grew unanimously and significantly: every one
of the 16 repositories with quality data was larger after adoption, a median
increase of 11\% within three months. Deletion activity also rose ($+24\%$,
$p=.007$) in 14 of 17 repositories.

Commit \emph{count} did not change ($p=.287$). Commit \emph{size} nearly
doubled in the median (244 $\to$ 436 lines added per commit) but is not
significant and splits 9/17 --- the median moved because a minority of
repositories changed a great deal, not because most did. That measure is also a
monthly mean ($\text{lines} \div \text{commits}$) rather than a distribution
over commits, so a single bulk import dominates its month; \texttt{nfiq}
2025-09 comprises 2 commits and \num{36810} lines. It should be read as
indicative only.

\subsection{Code quality}
\label{sec:quality}

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Code quality, raw totals.}
\label{tab:quality}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Measure (raw) & pre & post & ratio & $p$ & $n$ & up \\
\midrule
Cognitive complexity & \num{2703} & \num{2762} & 1.02 & \sig{.005} & 16 & \sig{14/16} \\
Code smells          & 790.5      & 827.5      & 1.05 & \sig{.011} & 16 & \sig{13/16} \\
Duplication (\%)     & 9.00       & 8.70       & 0.97 & \sig{.024} & 16 & 3/16 \\
Vulnerabilities      & 8.5        & 9.5        & 1.12 & .093 & 16 & 5/16 \\
Bugs                 & 14.0       & 14.0       & 1.00 & .063 & 16 & 6/16 \\
Technical debt (min) & \num{5554} & \num{5617} & 1.01 & .496 & 16 & 9/16 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Finding 2} Absolute complexity and code smells rose significantly:
complexity in 14 of 16 repositories ($+2\%$, $p=.005$) and smells in 13 of 16
($+5\%$, $p=.011$). Technical debt, bugs and vulnerabilities did not move.
Duplication \emph{fell} ($-3\%$, $p=.024$) in 13 of 16 repositories.

The increases are consistent in direction but small in magnitude. Read
alongside Finding~1, the same repositories over the same interval added
\textbf{11\% more code} while complexity rose \textbf{2\%} and smells
\textbf{5\%}: the measured quality burden grew, and grew more slowly than the
codebase producing it.

\paragraph{What absolute counts can and cannot settle.} A reviewer will ask
whether these increases reflect degrading code or simply more of it. Absolute
counts alone cannot separate the two: any codebase that grows accumulates more
complexity points and more smells even when every new line is written to the
same standard as the old. We report absolute values throughout, so the question
is raised here rather than answered. The relevant evidence available is the
juxtaposition above --- 11\% more code against a 2\% rise in complexity. A
size-conditioned analysis, or a model treating \texttt{ncloc} as a covariate,
would settle it directly and is the obvious next step.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/eventstudy.png}
  \caption{Event study on coding-agent adoption (purple band), all values raw.
  The earlier review-bot rollout appears as the dashed line at its median
  offset $\evt=-7$, with a band over its range. Axes are logarithmic where
  repositories differ by orders of magnitude. Box \emph{height} here is
  dominated by between-repository size rather than change over time; $n$
  beneath each box, red below 10.}
  \label{fig:eventstudy}
\end{figure*}

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/perrepo.png}
  \caption{Per-repository view, raw values, both axes anchored at zero. In most
  repositories the two lines track each other, the signature of complexity
  accumulating in step with code. The exceptions run both ways:
  \texttt{print} doubled in size (8k $\to$ 16k lines) with complexity almost
  flat (591 $\to$ 614), while \texttt{esignet-signup} grew 42\% in size
  (24k $\to$ 34k) but 88\% in complexity (\num{1543} $\to$ \num{2903}).}
  \label{fig:perrepo}
\end{figure*}

Per repository, 17 of 19 grew in size (Figure~\ref{fig:perrepo}). Both
directions of the complexity relationship are present and should not be
averaged away.

\subsection{Code-review practices}
\label{sec:review}

RQ3 is evaluated against \textbf{review-bot} adoption, the relevant
intervention, with pre $=\evt\in[-6,-1]$ and post $=\evt\in[+1,+6]$.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Review practice, split at review-bot adoption.}
\label{tab:review}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
Measure & pre & post & ratio & $p$ & $n$ \\
\midrule
AI reviews / month      & 0.0  & 6.0  & ---  & \sig{.001} & 19 \\
Human reviews / month   & 12.0 & 16.0 & 1.33 & .260       & 19 \\
All reviews / month     & 12.0 & 26.5 & 2.21 & \sig{.018} & 19 \\
\sig{Human reviews / PR}& \sig{1.43} & \sig{1.80} & 1.26 & \sig{.009} & 19 \\
PRs opened / month      & 12.5 & 8.0  & 0.64 & \sig{.006} & 19 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Finding 3} The review bot was \textbf{additive, not substitutive}.
Human review per pull request rose from \num{1.43} to \num{1.80} ($p=.009$),
falling in only 6 of 19 repositories. Total review activity more than doubled.
AI review accounts for \textbf{26.1\%} of all \num{15824} review submissions
across the organisation.

This is the clearest result in the study and the least threatened by the design
limitation in Section~\ref{sec:threats}, because the treatment indicator and
the outcome are the same class of event. The pre-adoption AI review share is
exactly zero in every repository in every month and rises to a plateau of
0.35--0.45 after adoption, which also validates the detection procedure end to
end.

\paragraph{A competing explanation.} Pull-request volume fell 36\% over the
same period ($12.5 \to 8.0$, $p=.006$). Human reviews per PR could therefore
rise because fewer, larger pull requests each attract more attention rather
than because reviewers became more engaged. Distinguishing these requires
conditioning on PR size, which we have not yet done.

%==============================================================================
\section{Threats to Validity}
\label{sec:threats}

\paragraph{Near-simultaneous adoption limits identification.} Fourteen of
nineteen repositories adopted CodeRabbit in a single month;
$\mathrm{corr}(\evt,t)=0.997$ for the review wave and $0.980$ for the coding
wave. In the canonical two-way specification
\begin{equation}
  y_{it} = \alpha_i + \lambda_t
         + \sum_{k \neq -1} \beta_k \mathbf{1}[\evt_{it}=k] + \varepsilon_{it},
  \label{eq:twfe}
\end{equation}
common adoption $a_i = a$ implies
$\mathbf{1}[\evt_{it}=k] = \mathbf{1}[t=a+k]$, a deterministic function of
calendar time lying in the span of the time fixed effects, so $\beta_k$ is not
separately identified. \textbf{The consequence is asymmetric}: a shared shock
can manufacture an apparent effect but cannot manufacture a null. Findings 2
and 3 are therefore more robust than any claim about activity levels.

\paragraph{Absolute counts confound quality with quantity.} Complexity and
smell totals necessarily rise in a growing codebase. Findings 1 and 2 report
real, separately measured quantities, but this study does not adjudicate
whether the quality increases exceed what growth alone implies.

\paragraph{An organisation-wide slowdown.} Mean output fell from \num{24.4} to
\num{14.5} commits per repository per month between 2025 and 2026, beginning
months before any coding agent. Every repository's pre/post boundary straddles
it. We therefore advance no velocity claim.

\paragraph{Short post-adoption window.} Coding agents arrived mid-2026 and the
data ends 2026-08. By $\evt=+3$ only six repositories remain. Long-run
maintainability effects --- precisely what the literature
predicts~\cite{he2026speed} --- are not yet observable.

\paragraph{Small treatment dose.} \num{147} commits are attributable to coding
agents across \num{326} repository-months.

\paragraph{Unbalanced panel.} Repositories enter and leave columns; only five
appear at both $\evt=-10$ and $\evt=+3$. Because all tests are
within-repository this does not bias them, but raw cross-column comparisons in
the figures are unsafe.

\paragraph{Single organisation.} MOSIP's engineering culture, review norms and
gitflow process are its own. Nothing here generalises without replication.

%==============================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Within one organisation, over a short post-adoption window, with a small
treatment dose: the code grew by 11\%, and its measured complexity and smell
counts grew by 2\% and 5\%. Technical debt, bugs and vulnerabilities did not
move; duplication improved. The direction is consistent with the population
literature~\cite{he2026speed}, but the magnitudes are substantially smaller
than the growth in code volume over the same interval, and this design cannot
separate either from a coincident organisational shock.

One contribution stands independently of the AI question. The distinction
between AI reviewers and AI writers is empirically consequential: these tools
arrived seven months apart, had different effects, and conflating them produces
attribution errors. The review-practice result in Section~\ref{sec:review} is
also the most robust finding here, because the outcome and the intervention are
the same class of event.

Next steps: construct a matched control cohort, which is the only remedy for
the identification problem in Section~\ref{sec:threats}; add a size-conditioned
analysis so Finding~2 can be read against what growth alone predicts; recompute
commit size as a true per-commit distribution, which also permits a
within-repository comparison of AI-authored against human-authored commits and
sidesteps the timing confound entirely; and re-run collection in six months,
when the post-adoption window will support the long-run question.

%==============================================================================
\section*{Data Availability}
All figures and statistics are reproducible from the per-repository reports and
the pooled panel \texttt{panel\_ai\_impact.csv} via the scripts in
\texttt{\_tools/analysis/}. Tests use \texttt{scipy.stats.wilcoxon},
two-sided, on repository-level pairs; percentiles use NumPy linear
interpolation (Hyndman--Fan type 7).

